---
import Guide from "../../layouts/Guide.astro"
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro"
import DocLink from "../../components/DocLink.astro"
import Example from "../../components/Example/index"
import RoleSwatches from "../../components/RoleSwatches.astro"
import TokenFlow from "../../components/TokenFlow.astro"

const surfaceRoles = [
  { name: "--ui-surface", note: "The page" },
  {
    name: "--ui-surface-elevated",
    note: "Elevated cards and accordions, dialogs, drawers, menus",
  },
  { name: "--ui-surface-tonal", note: "Tonal variants" },
  { name: "--ui-surface-filled", note: "Lists, tracks, table headers" },
  { name: "--ui-surface-inverse", note: "Tooltips" },
  { name: "--ui-text-strong", note: "Headings, labels, values" },
  { name: "--ui-text", note: "Body copy" },
  { name: "--ui-border-color", note: "Dividers, outlined variants" },
  { name: "--ui-field-border-color", note: "Fields" },
]
---

<Guide overline="Guide">
  <Fragment slot="title">Theming</Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="preamble">
    The knobs and roles behind colors, density, spacing, corners, fonts,
    elevation, motion and contrast, and the classes that scope them.
  </Fragment>

  <section>
    <p>
      A theme sets <DocLink href="/guide/concepts#token-tiers"
        >knobs and roles</DocLink
      >, and every component reads the roles:
    </p>
    <TokenFlow />
    <p>
      Snippets on this page belong in <code>@layer theme.custom</code>, see <DocLink
        href="/guide/customizing#your-theme">Your theme</DocLink
      >. Every token and its default is on <DocLink href="/guide/theme-tokens"
        >Theme tokens</DocLink
      >, and the <DocLink href="/theme-builder">Theme builder</DocLink> writes a theme
      for you and checks its contrast.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="palette">Palette</h2>
    <p>
      One hue and chroma in <code>theme.css</code> become a source color, <code
        >--ui-palette-source</code
      >, and <code>core/palette.css</code> derives the <code>--ui-color-1</code> to
      <code>--ui-color-16</code> steps from it.
    </p>

    <Code
      code={`:where(html) {
  --ui-palette-chroma: 0.5;
  --ui-palette-hue: 222.8;
  --ui-palette-hue-rotate-by: 0;
}`}
      lang="css"
    />

    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>--ui-palette-chroma</code> scales the saturation, from <code
          >0</code
        >
        (gray) to <code>1</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>--ui-palette-hue</code> is a hue angle in degrees, <code
          >222.8</code
        > (blue) by default.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>--ui-palette-hue-rotate-by</code> turns the hue by this many degrees
        per step, so <code>1</code> spreads 15° across the palette. The severity scopes
        use <code>1</code> for livelier tints.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Or set <code>--ui-palette-source</code> to any color: the palette keeps its
      oklch hue, scales its chroma and sets the lightness per step. The palette is
      computed on <code>:root</code>, <code>.ui-palette</code>, <code
        >.ui-contrast-more</code
      >, the severity classes and the inline <code>del</code>, <code>ins</code>,
      <code>abbr</code> and <code>dfn</code> elements and their classes, so the knobs
      do nothing anywhere else. Each severity class takes its hue from its intent
      token, so setting <code>--ui-critical</code>, <code>--ui-info</code>, <code
        >--ui-neutral</code
      >, <code>--ui-success</code> or <code>--ui-warning</code> recolors the scope.
      <code>.ui-neutral</code> also keeps the chroma of <code>--ui-neutral</code
      >, which follows <code>--ui-gray-hue</code> and <code
        >--ui-gray-chroma</code
      > by default:
    </p>

    <Code
      code={`:where(.ui-warning) {
  --ui-palette-source: oklch(from var(--ui-warning) 0.58 0.21 h);
  --ui-palette-hue-rotate-by: 1;
}`}
      lang="css"
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="scopes">Scopes</h2>
    <p>
      A scope class re-themes an element and everything inside it, so one page
      can carry several brands, a dark panel or one red for every critical
      component:
    </p>
    <Example centered row>
      <section
        class="ui-palette scope-demo"
        style="--ui-palette-hue: 30; --ui-palette-source: oklch(58% calc(0.21 * var(--ui-palette-chroma)) var(--ui-palette-hue)); --ui-primary-source: light-dark(var(--ui-color-9), var(--ui-color-6))"
      >
        <p class="ui-caption">.ui-palette with --ui-palette-hue: 30</p>
        <button class="ui-button ui-filled ui-primary" type="button">
          Primary
        </button>
        <button class="ui-button ui-tonal ui-primary" type="button">
          Tonal
        </button>
      </section>
      <aside class="ui-dark scope-demo">
        <p class="ui-caption">.ui-dark</p>
        <button class="ui-button ui-filled ui-primary" type="button">
          Primary
        </button>
        <button class="ui-button ui-tonal ui-primary" type="button">
          Tonal
        </button>
      </aside>
      <section class="scope-demo">
        <p class="ui-caption">.ui-critical on each</p>
        <article class="ui-callout ui-critical">
          <div class="ui-content">Card declined</div>
        </article>
        <button class="ui-button ui-tonal ui-critical" type="button">
          Remove card
        </button>
      </section>
    </Example>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>.ui-palette</code> re-derives the palette and every derived token (colors,
        control sizes, spacing, type roles and radii) from the knobs the element sets
        or inherits, <code>--ui-primary</code> and the intent colors included. <code
          >.ui-contrast-more</code
        > does the same with the contrast knobs tightened.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>.ui-critical</code>, <code>.ui-info</code>, <code
          >.ui-neutral</code
        >, <code>.ui-success</code> and <code>.ui-warning</code> swap the palette
        steps for the intent color and leave <code>--ui-primary</code> and the text
        colors alone. <DocLink href="/guide/concepts#palette-scopes"
          >Concepts</DocLink
        > shows the steps of each one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>.ui-light</code> and <code>.ui-dark</code> force a color scheme. On
        an element they only pick the other side of <code>light-dark()</code>:
        the surface and text colors and <code>--ui-shadow-elevated</code> switch,
        while the shadow color and strength still come from the page.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="surfaces">Surfaces</h2>
    <p>
      Five surfaces, two text colors and two border colors paint everything that
      isn't an accent:
    </p>
    <RoleSwatches roles={surfaceRoles} />
    <p>
      A raised component (tonal and elevated <code>Card</code> and <code
        >Accordion</code
      >, <code>Dialog</code>, <code>Drawer</code>, <code>Menu</code> and a <code
        >Callout</code
      > without a severity) sets <code>--ui-border-color</code> to <code
        >--ui-border-color-on-raised</code
      > and <code>--ui-field-border-color</code> to <code
        >--ui-field-border-color-on-raised</code
      > on its root, so dividers and fields inside it stay visible on the raised fill.
      Both default to the plain border colors. Filled lists and table headers do the
      same with the <code>-on-filled</code> pair. A theme that wants another border
      on raised surfaces sets the <code>-on-raised</code> tokens.
    </p>
    <p>
      The edge of the raised surface itself is <code
        >--ui-border-color-raised</code
      >, one word shorter. Every raised component reads it, the <code
        >Select</code
      > picker too, and each keeps its own edge until a theme sets it: cards, accordions
      and dialogs the page background, callouts their fill, drawers, menus and the
      picker <code>--ui-border-color-on-raised</code>. Set it to outline them
      all, or to <code>transparent</code> to drop their edges, like the m3 theme.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="density">Density</h2>
    <p>
      <code>--ui-density</code> scales the <code>--ui-control-size*</code> roles,
      so fields, buttons and list items grow or shrink together, and their padding
      shrinks down to the height of the text. Set it on <code>html</code>, or on
      a
      <code>.ui-palette</code> element to change one part of the page.
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`:where(html) {
  --ui-density: 0.875;
}`}
      lang="css"
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="spacing">Spacing</h2>
    <p>
      <code>--ui-space-1</code> to <code>--ui-space-8</code> pad and space containers,
      forms and rich text. Each is a fixed step (<code>0.25rem</code>, <code
        >0.5rem</code
      >, <code>1rem</code>, <code>1.25rem</code>, <code>1.5rem</code>, <code
        >1.75rem</code
      >, <code>2rem</code> and <code>3rem</code>) times
      <code>--ui-space-scale</code>, set on <code>html</code> or a <code
        >.ui-palette</code
      > element. Controls follow <code>--ui-density</code> instead, so a roomy form
      can keep compact fields.
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`:where(html) {
  --ui-space-scale: 1.25;
}

/* Or tie spacing to density */
:where(html) {
  --ui-density: 0.875;
  --ui-space-scale: var(--ui-density);
}`}
      lang="css"
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="corners">Corners</h2>
    <p>
      <code>--ui-radius</code> rounds every component, and buttons and fields follow
      it until you set <code>--ui-button-radius</code> or <code
        >--ui-field-radius</code
      >. Small parts like <code>kbd</code>, tooltips and checkboxes never get
      rounder than it, so <code>0</code> squares everything. <code
        >--ui-radius-x-small</code
      >, <code>--ui-radius-small</code> and <code>--ui-radius-large</code> derive
      from it (capped at 2px and 5px, and times 1.5), and <code
        >--ui-radius-round</code
      > is the pill.
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`:where(html) {
  --ui-radius: 0;
}`}
      lang="css"
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="fonts">Fonts</h2>
    <p>
      <code>--ui-font-body</code> sets the text and controls, <code
        >--ui-font-code</code
      > code, <code>kbd</code> and <code>samp</code>, and <code
        >--ui-font-heading</code
      > the headings, which follow the body font until you set it.
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`:where(html) {
  --ui-font-body: "Inter", system-ui, sans-serif;
  --ui-font-code: "JetBrains Mono", ui-monospace, monospace;
  --ui-font-heading: "Fraunces", serif;
}`}
      lang="css"
    />
    <p>
      The type scale, <code>--ui-font-size-100</code> to <code
        >--ui-font-size-900</code
      > (12px to 56px), is a primitive. Components read the roles on top of it, like
      <code>--ui-font-size-caption</code>, <code>--ui-font-size-control</code> and
      <code>--ui-font-size-h1</code>. Pin a role, move a step and every role on
      it follows, or replace the scale:
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`:where(html) {
  /* Pin one role */
  --ui-font-size-caption: 0.8125rem;

  /* Or a modular scale: 1rem base, minor third */
  --ui-font-size-100: calc(1rem * pow(1.2, -2));
  --ui-font-size-200: calc(1rem * pow(1.2, -1));
  --ui-font-size-300: 1rem;
  --ui-font-size-400: calc(1rem * pow(1.2, 1));
  --ui-font-size-500: calc(1rem * pow(1.2, 2));
  --ui-font-size-600: calc(1rem * pow(1.2, 3));
  --ui-font-size-700: calc(1rem * pow(1.2, 4));
  --ui-font-size-800: calc(1rem * pow(1.2, 5));
  --ui-font-size-900: calc(1rem * pow(1.2, 6));
}`}
      lang="css"
    />
    <h3 id="fluid-headings">Fluid headings</h3>
    <p>
      <code>--ui-font-size-h1</code> to <code>--ui-font-size-h4</code> grow from one
      step to the next as the viewport widens, together, over one window: <code
        >--ui-fluid-min-inline-size</code
      > (20rem) to <code>--ui-fluid-max-inline-size</code> (48rem). <code
        >--ui-fluid-progress</code
      > is the position in that window, 0 to 1, from <code>progress()</code>, so
      a theme can ramp other values on the same window. Move the window, reuse
      the ramp, or pin a heading to a step:
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`:where(html) {
  /* Headings reach full size on wider screens */
  --ui-fluid-min-inline-size: 24rem;
  --ui-fluid-max-inline-size: 64rem;

  /* Tighter spacing on narrow screens, on the same ramp */
  --ui-space-scale: calc(0.875 + 0.125 * var(--ui-fluid-progress));

  /* Or pin a heading */
  --ui-font-size-h1: var(--ui-font-size-800);
}`}
      lang="css"
    />
    <p>
      Browsers without <code>progress()</code> (before Firefox 155 and Safari 26)
      keep <code>clamp()</code> heading sizes and have no <code
        >--ui-fluid-progress</code
      >.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="elevation">Elevation</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>--ui-shadow-control</code>: buttons with a variant, chips, toggle
        buttons and fields. <code>none</code> until a theme sets it, and the optional
        <code>--ui-shadow-field</code> splits off the fields.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>--ui-shadow-elevated</code>: elevated cards and accordions.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>--ui-shadow-overlay</code>: dialogs, drawers, menus, <code
          >select</code
        >
        pickers and the carousel buttons.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>--ui-shadow-scroll</code>: the edges of scrolled dialogs, drawers
        and sticky table headers.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>--ui-shadow-surface</code>: tonal and outlined cards and
        accordions, and callouts. <code>none</code> by default.
      </li>
      <li><code>--ui-shadow-selected</code>: the selected tab.</li>
      <li>
        <code>--ui-shadow-color</code>: the color and base opacity every default
        shadow is built from, a <code>light-dark()</code> pair, so shadows darken
        in dark mode, also in <code>.ui-dark</code> parts of the page. Each layer
        adds its own opacity on top.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Code
      code={`:where(html) {
  --ui-shadow-color: light-dark(oklch(0.28 0.055 290 / 2%), oklch(0 0 0 / 30%));
  --ui-shadow-elevated: none;
}`}
      lang="css"
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="motion">Motion</h2>
    <p>
      <code>--ui-motion</code> multiplies every duration. It is <code>1</code>,
      or
      <code>0</code> under <code>prefers-reduced-motion: reduce</code>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="motion-global">Global classes</h3>
    <p>
      On <code>html</code>, these override the OS preference for every
      transition and animation. Smooth scrolling follows the system setting
      only, since <code>normalize.css</code> keys it on <code
        >prefers-reduced-motion</code
      >.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>.ui-motion-off</code>: sets <code>--ui-motion: 0</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>.ui-motion-on</code>: sets <code>--ui-motion: 1</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>.ui-motion-debug</code>: sets <code>--ui-motion: 10</code> (slows down
        transitions 10x).
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Code code={`<html lang="en" class="ui-motion-debug">`} lang="html" />

    <h3 id="motion-local">Local overrides</h3>
    <p>
      Each component reads it through its own <code>--_motion</code>, so you can
      turn one off:
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`<button class="ui-button" style="--_motion: 0" type="button">
  Instant interaction
</button>`}
      lang="html"
    />

    <h3 id="motion-personality">Springy movement</h3>
    <p>
      Two optional tokens give the controls some personality. <code
        >--ui-ease-move</code
      > is the easing of things that move: the checkbox check, the radio dot and the
      switch thumb as they turn on, the tabs indicator, the press scale, and menus,
      selects, tooltips and toasts as they open. Nothing closing reads it.
      <code>--ui-press-scale</code> shrinks buttons, toggle buttons and chips while
      they're pressed. Both are unset, so nothing bounces or shrinks until a theme
      sets them, and both follow <code>--ui-motion</code>.
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`:root {
  --ui-ease-move: linear(0, 0.066, 0.218, 0.4, 0.578, 0.731, 0.852, 0.94, 0.998,
    1.031, 1.047, 1.05, 1.046, 1.038, 1.029, 1.02, 1.012, 1.007, 1.002, 1, 1);
  --ui-press-scale: 0.97;
}`}
      lang="css"
    />

    <p>To follow it in your own CSS:</p>
    <Code
      code={`transition: transform calc(var(--ui-duration) * var(--ui-motion)) var(--ui-ease);`}
      lang="css"
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="contrast">Contrast</h2>
    <p>
      <code>--ui-contrast</code> is <code>normal</code>, or <code>more</code> under
      <code>prefers-contrast: more</code>. More contrast changes no color
      directly. It tightens the knobs the roles are derived from, and every role
      follows:
      <code>--ui-text-strength-light</code> and <code
        >--ui-text-strength-dark</code
      >
      (how much of <code>--ui-text-strong</code> goes into <code>--ui-text</code
      >),
      <code>--ui-border-strength-light</code>, <code
        >--ui-border-strength-dark</code
      >
      and <code>--ui-field-border-strength</code> for the borders, <code
        >--ui-text-max-lightness</code
      >, <code>--ui-text-min-lightness</code> and <code
        >--ui-text-max-chroma</code
      >
      for the <code>-text</code> roles, <code
        >--ui-accent-fill-max-lightness</code
      > for intent fills, <code>--ui-primary-max-lightness</code> and <code
        >--ui-primary-min-lightness</code
      > for the primary, the <code>--ui-palette-*-lightness</code> limits for the
      palette roles, and <code>--ui-focus-ring-width</code>. Their values in
      both modes are on
      <DocLink href="/guide/theme-tokens#contrast">Theme tokens</DocLink>.
      Components with translucent fills or text have style queries of their own
      for the shape changes.
    </p>
    <p>
      A theme that sets a knob or <code>--ui-primary-source</code> keeps working with
      more contrast. The example themes set their brand color that way and let
      <code>--ui-on-primary</code> and <code>--ui-primary-text</code> derive from
      it, so Bootstrap's blue darkens under more contrast and its text follows. A
      theme that pins a role directly, such as <code
        >--ui-border-color: oklch(0.95 0 0)</code
      >, owns that role in every contrast mode. Set the knob instead, or hand
      the role back to the library under more contrast with <code
        >revert-layer</code
      >, which drops the theme's value and keeps the library's formula, computed
      from the theme's own <code>--ui-text-strong</code> and <code
        >--ui-surface</code
      >:
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`@layer theme.custom {
  :where(html, .ui-palette, .ui-contrast-more) {
    --ui-border-color: oklch(0.95 0 0);
  }

  @media (prefers-contrast: more) {
    :where(
      html:not(.ui-contrast-normal),
      html:not(.ui-contrast-normal) .ui-palette
    ) {
      --ui-border-color: revert-layer;
    }
  }

  :where(.ui-contrast-more, .ui-contrast-more .ui-palette) {
    --ui-border-color: revert-layer;
  }
}`}
      lang="css"
    />
    <p>
      The example themes do this for their borders, field borders, focus ring
      and subtle text, so they keep their originals' look at normal contrast and
      meet every contrast promise with more contrast. Hand back a role only
      where the library's value is stronger: a theme whose <code>--ui-text</code
      > already equals <code>--ui-text-strong</code> keeps it, and brutalist, whose
      borders are already black, draws them in <code>--ui-text</code> instead so they
      stay visible in dark mode. The Theme builder hands back the same roles when
      you set them.
    </p>

    <Example row showCode={false}>
      <div class="contrast-demo">
        <section>
          <p class="ui-caption">Default</p>
          <label class="ui-text-field">
            <span class="ui-label">Email</span>
            <span class="ui-field">
              <input type="email" placeholder="name@example.com" />
            </span>
            <span class="ui-end-text">We'll never share it.</span>
          </label>
          <button class="ui-button ui-primary ui-outlined" type="button">
            Subscribe
          </button>
        </section>
        <section class="ui-contrast-more">
          <p class="ui-caption">.ui-contrast-more</p>
          <label class="ui-text-field">
            <span class="ui-label">Email</span>
            <span class="ui-field">
              <input type="email" placeholder="name@example.com" />
            </span>
            <span class="ui-end-text">We'll never share it.</span>
          </label>
          <button class="ui-button ui-primary ui-outlined" type="button">
            Subscribe
          </button>
        </section>
      </div>
    </Example>

    <h3 id="contrast-classes">Classes</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>.ui-contrast-more</code>: sets <code>--ui-contrast: more</code> and
        the tightened knobs, on <code>html</code> or any element. On an element it
        is a scope like <code>.ui-palette</code>, so every role inside is
        derived again.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>.ui-contrast-normal</code>: sets <code>--ui-contrast: normal</code
        >, on <code>html</code> to ignore the OS preference. It can't lower the contrast
        inside a <code>.ui-contrast-more</code> ancestor.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Code code={`<html lang="en" class="ui-contrast-more">`} lang="html" />

    <h3 id="contrast-custom">Custom values</h3>
    <p>
      To tune more contrast, set the knobs where the library sets them: on <code
        >html</code
      > under the media query, and on <code>.ui-contrast-more</code>.
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`@layer theme.custom {
  @media (prefers-contrast: more) {
    :where(html:not(.ui-contrast-normal)) {
      --ui-border-strength-light: 70%;
      --ui-field-border-strength: 80%;
    }
  }

  :where(.ui-contrast-more) {
    --ui-border-strength-light: 70%;
    --ui-field-border-strength: 80%;
  }
}`}
      lang="css"
    />

    <h3 id="forced-colors">Forced colors</h3>
    <p>
      Under <code>forced-colors: active</code>, selected tabs, toggles and list
      items use <code>SelectedItem</code>, switches, ranges, progress bars and
      dividers
      <code>CanvasText</code>, and focused fields a <code>Highlight</code> outline.
      Nothing to configure.
    </p>
  </section>
</Guide>

<style>
  .scope-demo {
    align-items: start;
    border-radius: var(--ui-radius);
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--ui-space-3);
    padding: var(--ui-space-4);

    .ui-caption {
      margin: 0;
    }
  }

  .contrast-demo {
    display: grid;
